[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was marked known, with one issue and one fix queued. Its reports describe
two problems, and they ended differently. Engineering closed #4471 on 5 October.

<Callout>
The parser is fixed. The hint is not fixed: booking-v3's form was retired instead.
</Callout>

## The problems

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {tickets} />

- **`parser`:** the shared parser reads every date month first, so tenants that write the day first
  get the day and month swapped. Fixed by `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`.
- **`hint`:** booking-v3's form tells people to type `MM/DD/YYYY`, then reads what they type the
  tenant's way. Only reports through that form belong here. The same tenants' app and email reports
  are `parser`. Closed as won't fix, because the form was retired.

## How the reports were counted

Each report is placed by its tenant and way in, using `causes.ts`, and repeated export rows count
once. Intake's tag was not used, because it follows the reporter's browser and not the tenant.

The note under the table lists the reports the desk read as a one-day move. They stay under `parser`,
as the desk files them by tenant, but their text does not describe a swap. A time zone cause was not
confirmed or ruled out, and the export ends before the fix, so check whether they stop now that it has shipped.

## How they came to be merged

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges reports on subject line alone, and
reporters describe both problems with the same few subjects. The rule accepts that one bug may
get several tickets. It did not consider one ticket hiding several bugs.

## What the desk should do

- Watch new reports for dates that still move by one day. If they continue, open a new ticket.
- Check that no tenant still takes bookings through booking-v3's form, because the hint bug remains in it.
- Write a new decision record to replace 0001, one that merges on cause. The
  [triage page](/kb/triage/dedupe) follows 0001 and would change with it.

